1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,600
The world has always had its share of dreamers and schemers, tinkerers and inventors.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:12,200
Some who've had great ideas, but many more who've missed the mark, been ahead of their times,

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,700
or whose ideas were just plain bad.

4
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:20,200
This is a nod to the off-kilter geniuses who've come up short on success.

5
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,700
Now, Failed Inventions on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,160
When it comes to failed inventions, bad ideas run the gamut.

7
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:46,740
But in all likelihood, no other single invention has spawned more failed spin-offs than the automobile.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,460
Individual tinkerers and major car manufacturers alike have always tried to come up with new and exciting ways to get a car to take us where we want to go.

9
00:00:56,461 --> 00:01:03,460
The 1950s were a period of growing prosperity in the United States.

10
00:01:03,461 --> 00:01:07,460
You had a lot of confidence in modernity and the future.

11
00:01:07,461 --> 00:01:15,460
And this was a really kind of car-crazy time when a lot of people's fantasies were invested in their automobiles.

12
00:01:15,461 --> 00:01:21,460
And a lot of manufacturers tried to satisfy that whoosh fulfillment.

13
00:01:21,461 --> 00:01:25,460
And that's, I think, why we saw some of these rather bizarre concepts come along.

14
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:32,600
In the 1960s, the Ford Motor Company was eager to show the public it was on the cutting edge of innovative design

15
00:01:32,601 --> 00:01:36,480
and actually toyed with the notion of building a nuclear-powered engine.

16
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:42,580
The nucleon, as it was called, would have come complete with its own plutonium pod.

17
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:49,080
But questions of where to refuel and the potential dangers of having a rolling nuclear reactor

18
00:01:49,081 --> 00:01:52,940
kept the nucleon from making it past this three-foot model stage.

19
00:01:56,460 --> 00:02:07,286
A more serious experiment began in 1954, when the Chrysler Corporation embarked on a nearly 30-year odyssey to create a car whose power plant would borrow jet-age

20
00:02:07,287 --> 00:02:10,160
technology to improve power and efficiency.

21
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:13,460
A car with the power plant of a jet engine.

22
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:21,140
October 1963 saw the arrival of Chrysler's new turbine-powered car.

23
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:26,360
Engines built by American engineers were sheathed in a European-styled body,

24
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,340
co-designed with the Ghia Corporation's top battalion designers.

25
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:39,700
With 80% fewer parts than the standard car engine, Chrysler was also promoting the turbine's simple design and reliability.

26
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:46,500
Here is full power potential for instant acceleration and outstanding climbing ability.

27
00:02:46,501 --> 00:02:52,600
The driver is assured the same control he can get in the best conventional passenger

28
00:02:52,601 --> 00:02:58,940
car, even on the steepest grades.

29
00:02:58,941 --> 00:03:02,720
The car's engine was surprisingly simple in design.

30
00:03:02,721 --> 00:03:06,440
Air would be drawn into the Chrysler's compressor where temperature and air pressure would be

31
00:03:06,441 --> 00:03:08,940
dramatically increased.

32
00:03:08,941 --> 00:03:15,180
The air would then be forced into a burner where it would mix with fuel creating combustion.

33
00:03:15,181 --> 00:03:20,700
gases would then expand through a series of turbine wheels to transform thermal energy

34
00:03:20,701 --> 00:03:25,260
into mechanical energy to turn the car's wheels.

35
00:03:25,261 --> 00:03:29,500
Power and handling for the Chrysler turbine was equal to anything else on the road.

36
00:03:29,501 --> 00:03:35,440
However, acceleration was sluggish due to the turbine's lag in engaging the transmission.

37
00:03:35,441 --> 00:03:38,760
But the Chrysler turbine did have one fascinating feature.

38
00:03:38,761 --> 00:03:40,620
ran on any combustible liquid.

39
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:45,620
Gasoline, perfume, or even corn oil, all worked equally well.

40
00:03:46,220 --> 00:03:48,260
There's an actual story that at the Proving Grounds,

41
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:51,700
they had poured scotch into the tank to see if they could make it run that way.

42
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:55,500
When they were done running, a couple of the salty old mechanics in the garage

43
00:03:55,501 --> 00:03:57,620
were very unhappy that they wasted scotch in such a manner.

44
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:01,340
The project was ultimately deemed unfeasible,

45
00:04:01,341 --> 00:04:04,800
largely because production costs would have been too high for consumers.

46
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:11,680
Research in new metal alloys able to withstand the turbine's high operating temperatures was also lagging.

47
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:14,760
Fifty cars were built by Ghia of Italy in Turin.

48
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:17,620
They sent these to the United States for evaluation.

49
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:20,460
They were all fitted with the motor here in the United States.

50
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:27,560
After seven years, when the program was done, due to tax and customs reasons, all but nine of the vehicles were destroyed.

51
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,020
Chrysler finally abandoned the turbine engine concept in 1981.

52
00:04:32,021 --> 00:04:41,620
But surely the most unusual failed car design was the product of a German amphibious aircraft designer, Hans Tripler.

53
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:48,320
The Amphic car came out in the mid-60s when the Bond movies were big,

54
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:55,120
and the notion that you could just head into the water to escape pursuit had a kind of escapist appeal.

55
00:04:55,121 --> 00:05:03,100
For nine years, beginning in 1960, Tripler exported nearly 3,000 of his Amphicars to the U.S.

56
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:08,500
Each Amphicar came with a standard four-cylinder engine and its own bilge pump.

57
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:13,580
A false bottom floor protects electrical wiring and gives added protection from leaks.

58
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:18,900
A dash-mounted switch engages the transmission-powered dual propellers.

59
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:23,480
Good work, man. Do you have any brake for Paul?

60
00:05:23,481 --> 00:05:32,240
The Amphicar handles primarily like a car in the water, but consequently it handles pretty much like a boat on land.

61
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:36,600
The twin propellers in the rear turn them in the same direction.

62
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:39,945
Only one way can you turn pretty efficiently.

63
00:05:39,946 --> 00:05:42,340
The other way turns kind of like the Queen Mary.

64
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:47,360
Costing nearly the same amount as a Mustang, the public faced a tough choice.

65
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:52,040
Cool muscle car or ugly duckling that was all wet.

66
00:05:52,041 --> 00:05:55,280
the Mustang won the popularity contest

67
00:05:55,281 --> 00:05:57,860
the legacy will be

68
00:05:57,861 --> 00:05:59,740
a great idea

69
00:05:59,741 --> 00:06:03,880
that never was popularized enough

70
00:06:03,881 --> 00:06:07,380
and so never took off

71
00:06:07,381 --> 00:06:16,480
but the biggest flops in the auto industry

72
00:06:16,481 --> 00:06:19,440
have come at the hands of giant sized personalities

73
00:06:19,441 --> 00:06:22,300
chasing their dream of building the perfect car.

74
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:28,440
A former Ford designer, Preston Tucker was one of those dreamers.

75
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:32,300
His plan was grand, some said foolhardy,

76
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:35,040
to build a car company from the ground up.

77
00:06:36,340 --> 00:06:38,880
Preston Tucker was, above all, a salesman.

78
00:06:39,160 --> 00:06:41,600
And like all salesmen, the first thing he sold was himself.

79
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:44,520
And he was a man with lots of ideas.

80
00:06:44,521 --> 00:06:46,860
He was always promoting these ideas.

81
00:06:46,861 --> 00:06:54,560
After the war, when the market was exploding, he thought that he could build an automobile.

82
00:06:55,780 --> 00:07:03,800
Starting an automobile company from scratch in 1948 was actually a virtually impossible task

83
00:07:03,801 --> 00:07:09,320
because the capital required was so huge that nobody could come up with it.

84
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:14,700
Tucker's vision for his four-wheeled namesake was to build the safest car on the road.

85
00:07:14,701 --> 00:07:18,020
He designed this car, a rear-engine car.

86
00:07:18,300 --> 00:07:25,200
He put a lot of emphasis on safety, making a crush-proof passenger compartment and a pop-out windshield.

87
00:07:26,040 --> 00:07:33,180
And the idea was that in an accident, when you went forward into the windshield, the windshield would pop out.

88
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:35,440
And you wouldn't cut yourself.

89
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:43,780
The Tucker's most memorable feature was the triclops, a third headlight that turned with the steering wheel to continuously light the road ahead.

90
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:52,720
Long before a single car was produced, the savvy Tucker was conjuring ways to generate buzz for his dream car.

91
00:07:53,580 --> 00:07:57,040
One of the ways to promote the car was to build the radio for the car.

92
00:07:57,420 --> 00:08:00,200
And you could go in and you could buy the radio for your Tucker.

93
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:06,160
And to make sure that you were always aware that that was a radio for your Tucker,

94
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:13,140
why the buttons, the push buttons on the radio had the letters T-U-C-K-E-R, one on each button.

95
00:08:14,700 --> 00:08:19,900
But when Tucker began selling stock in his fledgling enterprise, his dream began to evaporate.

96
00:08:20,980 --> 00:08:27,680
Once word spread that the government was investigating Tucker's business practices, investors' money quickly dried up.

97
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:33,180
It's safe to say that more shares in Tucker's company were sold than actual Tucker automobiles.

98
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:36,900
In all, only 50 cars were ever built.

99
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:44,460
Another man who shared a passion for elevating the automobile's design was Edsel Ford, Henry's son.

100
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:50,800
Killed during World War II, the Ford Company wanted to honor him by attaching his name to their newest project.

101
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:55,060
Up to now, we at Ford have called it simply the E-car.

102
00:08:55,940 --> 00:09:01,160
Today, it gives me a great deal of pleasure to announce its official name, Edsel,

103
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:06,040
in honor of my father, who served as president of Ford Motor Company.

104
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:12,040
The Edsel has, of course, gone down in history as a synonym for failure.

105
00:09:12,041 --> 00:09:18,900
and it's it's unfortunate first of all because the edsel with its rather odd name is actually

106
00:09:18,901 --> 00:09:24,860
named after henry ford's only son some people think it was ugly other people just think it

107
00:09:24,861 --> 00:09:31,360
looked funny the irony is that edsel ford after whom the car is named was a man of impeccable

108
00:09:31,361 --> 00:09:37,100
taste and he was the inspiration for what is regarded as one of the most beautiful cars of

109
00:09:37,101 --> 00:09:45,020
all time the original mark one lincoln continental among the things that the edsel was immediately

110
00:09:45,021 --> 00:09:52,460
ridiculed for was its distinctive what they called the horse collar grill but which became more

111
00:09:52,461 --> 00:09:59,860
commonly known as the toilet seat grill only three years after its debut ford pulled the edsel from

112
00:09:59,861 --> 00:10:05,220
its lineup estimates place ford's cost from marketing and building the edsel at somewhere

113
00:10:05,221 --> 00:10:08,960
between 250 to 350 million dollars.

114
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:11,700
The industry's biggest flop.

115
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:15,260
There's no telling what new inventions

116
00:10:15,261 --> 00:10:16,840
will come rolling down the highway

117
00:10:16,841 --> 00:10:17,720
in the coming years.

118
00:10:18,220 --> 00:10:21,000
But perhaps the future of automotive transportation

119
00:10:21,001 --> 00:10:22,680
lies in the skies above.

120
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:25,880
Next, cars that fly

121
00:10:25,881 --> 00:10:28,220
and men that soar without wings.

122
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:31,860
Bill Lear, inventor of the Learjet,

123
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:33,680
car radio and 8-track stereo,

124
00:10:33,681 --> 00:10:39,120
spent the last 10 years of his life failing to create a practical steam-powered car.

125
00:10:40,140 --> 00:10:42,580
Failed Inventions will return on Modern Marvels.

126
00:10:45,780 --> 00:10:49,320
The Wright brothers built the world's first successful airplane

127
00:10:49,321 --> 00:10:52,500
here at their bike shop in December of 1903.

128
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:57,120
And since that fateful day, there has been no shortage of failed attempts

129
00:10:57,121 --> 00:11:00,320
to invent a different, if not better, kind of flying machine.

130
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:06,460
Keep your eye on the birdie.

131
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:08,360
Cuckoo. Cuckoo.

132
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:14,320
By mid-century, America's love affair with the car was in full bloom,

133
00:11:14,740 --> 00:11:17,740
and marrying the airplane and automobile seemed a perfect tonic

134
00:11:17,741 --> 00:11:21,120
for quickly and easily moving a world of adventurous souls.

135
00:11:21,540 --> 00:11:24,720
I think there's just something in the crackpot adventure

136
00:11:24,721 --> 00:11:29,180
that loves the idea of marrying two things that don't really go together.

137
00:11:29,181 --> 00:11:34,780
There are a number of flying car attempts over the years.

138
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:37,280
Recently, someone tried to make a flying Pinto,

139
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:44,220
which with its history of fuel tank explosions would seem to be doubly alarming.

140
00:11:44,660 --> 00:11:49,060
More than 75 patents for flying cars have been issued since 1917,

141
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:55,640
but only one, Malt Taylor's 1949 Aero car, attained any lasting notoriety.

142
00:11:56,620 --> 00:11:59,660
Moulton Taylor was inspired to build an aero car

143
00:11:59,661 --> 00:12:03,120
because he believed that in the period after World War II,

144
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:06,420
there were lots of pilots and we had very congested highways.

145
00:12:07,060 --> 00:12:09,560
Very possibly, he could develop a flying car

146
00:12:09,561 --> 00:12:11,880
that would alleviate some of that ground congestion

147
00:12:11,881 --> 00:12:13,480
in the form of a flying car.

148
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:19,020
With the flying car, you had necessary compromises.

149
00:12:19,021 --> 00:12:21,920
The car itself was not particularly roadworthy.

150
00:12:22,420 --> 00:12:25,340
It wasn't heavy enough to be safe on the road.

151
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:29,040
When you just put wings on this thing, you have a rather clunky airplane.

152
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:33,940
The notion that just ordinary drivers would be flying around,

153
00:12:33,941 --> 00:12:39,340
particularly over major cities, is alarming in itself.

154
00:12:39,780 --> 00:12:43,120
Same guys that cut you off on the highway at 65 miles an hour

155
00:12:43,121 --> 00:12:46,820
would instead be cutting you off at 10,000 feet.

156
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:49,920
Still, the AeroCar had some unique features.

157
00:12:50,820 --> 00:12:54,100
It was the first U.S. rear-engined car with a front-wheel drive.

158
00:12:55,080 --> 00:12:57,800
An aft-mounted driveshaft powered the pusher prop,

159
00:12:57,980 --> 00:13:00,940
while the rear wheels were allowed to freely spin,

160
00:13:01,380 --> 00:13:03,300
a critical feature for smooth landings.

161
00:13:03,980 --> 00:13:08,680
An earlier version of a flying car, Robert Fulton's 1946 Air Fibian,

162
00:13:09,180 --> 00:13:12,140
had a single cumbersome fuselage and wing component

163
00:13:12,141 --> 00:13:14,620
that necessitated stowage at local airports.

164
00:13:14,621 --> 00:13:18,280
Unfortunately, it's an invention whose time has not yet come,

165
00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:20,740
and it never really gets off the ground.

166
00:13:20,741 --> 00:13:27,360
Malt Taylor's aero car on the other hand strove to keep travel flexible with its foldable and

167
00:13:27,361 --> 00:13:30,486
towable wings. Lying into a storm?

168
00:13:30,487 --> 00:13:35,360
Find a runway, land, tow your wings to clearer skies,

169
00:13:35,361 --> 00:13:37,922
and resume your airborne journey.

170
00:13:37,923 --> 00:13:41,960
While its lines aren't particularly aerodynamic, the

171
00:13:41,961 --> 00:13:45,459
aero car required only a standard small craft runway.

172
00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:48,100
Still, red tape kept the aero car mostly

173
00:13:48,101 --> 00:13:48,859
grounded.

174
00:13:48,860 --> 00:13:55,180
The market is too small to build a flying car where you have to be a pilot,

175
00:13:56,040 --> 00:14:02,260
a driver, you have to have an FAA medical, you have to have all the other licenses to operate

176
00:14:02,261 --> 00:14:03,946
radios and things like that.

177
00:14:03,947 --> 00:14:07,980
A high price tag and the inconvenience of attaching and reattaching

178
00:14:07,981 --> 00:14:12,705
wings also helped kill the dream of flying cars for the masses.

179
00:14:12,706 --> 00:14:14,880
Only five aero cars were ever

180
00:14:14,881 --> 00:14:19,160
built, leaving us with only the fantasy of flying out of a rush hour jam.

181
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:27,500
Man being powered through the air without benefit of plane or balloon is today a reality.

182
00:14:28,180 --> 00:14:30,840
Tomorrow, he may even be a traffic problem.

183
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:39,300
For many, the 1960s rocket belt remains the most intriguing flying invention that failed.

184
00:14:40,300 --> 00:14:42,300
There were several incarnations over the years.

185
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:44,660
The first was built by the Bell Jet Labs.

186
00:14:44,880 --> 00:14:54,804
The original intent of the hydrogen-powered rocket belt was to provide soldiers with a means for aerial reconnaissance, ease of travel over difficult terrain, and a

187
00:14:54,805 --> 00:14:56,680
stable weapons firing platform.

188
00:14:57,400 --> 00:14:59,960
The rocket belt failed on all counts.

189
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:12,660
It was too loud to provide safe spying, held only enough fuel for 21-second trips, and was too complex to fly and fire weapons at the same time.

190
00:15:12,661 --> 00:15:18,860
The most famous version was designed and built by famed Hollywood cameraman Nelson Tyler,

191
00:15:19,380 --> 00:15:21,900
whose rocket belt flew over the 1984 Olympics.

192
00:15:22,520 --> 00:15:26,100
The whole thing is controlled with these arms that come out.

193
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:27,920
You've got a throttle to go up and down.

194
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:29,720
Move the stick up and down.

195
00:15:29,860 --> 00:15:31,400
You fly forward and aft.

196
00:15:32,020 --> 00:15:33,580
Sideways, you twist this.

197
00:15:33,940 --> 00:15:36,100
The fun thing, it's pretty hard to fly.

198
00:15:36,101 --> 00:15:40,080
It's hard to learn and train because you only have 20 seconds at a time to do it.

199
00:15:40,081 --> 00:15:43,000
If the rocket belt fails, you just fall like a brick,

200
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:47,820
except you fall with this thing on your back and all this fuel so that when you crash, you burn, you know.

201
00:15:48,620 --> 00:15:54,380
The rocket belt is powered by a specially formulated 90% pure hydrogen peroxide mix.

202
00:15:55,700 --> 00:15:58,500
Forced under pressure through a series of fine silver meshes,

203
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:05,220
a chemical reaction is produced that creates heat, water, and 300 pounds of steam-powered thrust.

204
00:16:05,221 --> 00:16:13,300
The military abandoned the rocket belt in the 1960s, unable to develop lighter fuels for longer flight times.

205
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:20,820
But the sight of this strange contraption has made imagination soar with the possibility of Superman-like wingless flight.

206
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:30,480
Of all the items in our stock catalog, there is only one which would have the high velocity and low wind resistance of flying saucers.

207
00:16:30,481 --> 00:16:35,340
the cover of a GI trash can.

208
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:43,000
For years, people have sworn that flying saucers have appeared around Nevada's infamous Area 51,

209
00:16:43,540 --> 00:16:46,280
home of several supposed UFO sightings.

210
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:49,640
There might be some truth to those rumors.

211
00:16:51,100 --> 00:16:56,185
Meet the Avro, a two-seater flying disc built in 1958 by the U.S.

212
00:16:56,186 --> 00:16:59,080
Air Force and Canadian manufacturers.

213
00:16:59,081 --> 00:17:06,160
trying to capitalize on emerging jet technology the u.s military spent nearly 12 million dollars

214
00:17:06,161 --> 00:17:13,420
on the futuristic avro intended to hover at altitudes greater than 10 000 feet the three

215
00:17:13,421 --> 00:17:21,360
turbine fans generated enough lift for a pilot and his passenger the 927 pounds of thrust from

216
00:17:21,361 --> 00:17:28,080
the continental turbo power plants was directed downward via internal shafts to create a hovercraft

217
00:17:28,081 --> 00:17:28,781
effect.

218
00:17:28,782 --> 00:17:33,640
The Avro was also equipped with a special side-mounting venting system to provide additional

219
00:17:33,641 --> 00:17:35,873
lateral control to prevent tipping.

220
00:17:35,874 --> 00:17:39,700
Engineers were never able to completely control the unwieldy

221
00:17:39,701 --> 00:17:44,820
Avro during untethered flights or get it to lift more than several inches off the ground.

222
00:17:46,060 --> 00:17:51,780
Barely able to cross small ditches, the Air Force's hope for an all-terrain vehicle was dashed.

223
00:17:51,781 --> 00:17:58,800
Hovering disks, flying cars, and flying rocket men have all but disappeared from the scene.

224
00:17:59,240 --> 00:18:03,440
But some of the biggest flops have come at the hands of those with the most success.

225
00:18:04,020 --> 00:18:09,180
Up next, Thomas Edison gets bogged down in houses made of cement.

226
00:18:10,380 --> 00:18:16,840
The first commercial jetliner, the Comet, failed because its square-shaped windows caused deadly cracks in the fuselage.

227
00:18:17,700 --> 00:18:20,200
Failed inventions will return on Modern Marvels.

228
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:25,440
Most failed inventions are the brainchilds of anonymous creators.

229
00:18:26,420 --> 00:18:28,040
But they're in good company.

230
00:18:28,880 --> 00:18:30,840
Even great minds have bad ideas.

231
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:36,500
Thomas Edison, Nikola Tesla, and futurist Buckminster Fuller

232
00:18:36,501 --> 00:18:40,100
have all laid eggs during their otherwise successful careers.

233
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:44,180
Anyone who's really thinking out of the box,

234
00:18:44,380 --> 00:18:46,560
who is pushing themselves constantly,

235
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:51,620
is going to kick out a few ideas that just simply don't work.

236
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:59,520
Nikola Tesla is remembered as the champion of electricity's alternating current, the X-ray, and wireless communication.

237
00:19:00,260 --> 00:19:02,600
He created the power station at Niagara Falls.

238
00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:08,400
And in an era of the horse and buggy, Tesla was creating radio-guided torpedoes.

239
00:19:09,140 --> 00:19:13,160
But many wonder if some of his inventions came from an even darker place.

240
00:19:14,520 --> 00:19:19,900
Tesla would have to be considered one of the quintessential mad scientists of all time.

241
00:19:19,901 --> 00:19:24,399
Tesla was said to have been engaged in designing a death ray for the U.S.

242
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:26,680
government just prior to World War I.

243
00:19:27,640 --> 00:19:30,260
Generating more than 50 million volts of electricity,

244
00:19:30,820 --> 00:19:36,540
the super-concentrated beam of energy would theoretically have been powerful enough to destroy entire cities.

245
00:19:37,560 --> 00:19:41,460
The idea seemed outlandish, but with Tesla, you never knew.

246
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:46,040
He was the kind of guy that you didn't want to dismiss too lightly

247
00:19:46,041 --> 00:19:52,660
because he had achieved enough of his theories that he had quite a bit of credibility.

248
00:19:53,440 --> 00:19:58,200
Tesla also imagined a day in which airplanes would be powered not by engines,

249
00:19:58,760 --> 00:20:01,660
but by powerful electromagnetic beams of energy.

250
00:20:02,340 --> 00:20:04,880
Broadcast from towers across the United States,

251
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:09,360
airplanes would be pushed along their journey via small belly-mounted receptors.

252
00:20:10,340 --> 00:20:16,320
Tesla went so far as to build a giant 175-foot electrical tower on Long Island.

253
00:20:17,160 --> 00:20:20,140
But funding fell through before tests could be conducted.

254
00:20:21,020 --> 00:20:26,260
Although he conquered Niagara Falls, Tesla's genius didn't extend to his personal finances.

255
00:20:27,100 --> 00:20:32,960
He died nearly penniless, unable to test the countless ideas he scribbled in his notebooks each day.

256
00:20:33,940 --> 00:20:38,340
Researchers still scour his writings, hoping to uncover some new kernel of genius.

257
00:20:39,360 --> 00:20:43,600
Even the great inventive mind of Thomas Edison hatched some duds.

258
00:20:44,460 --> 00:20:47,300
The father of the light bulb, phonograph, and motion picture,

259
00:20:47,940 --> 00:20:52,460
Edison was granted an astounding 400 patents in a prolific eight-year period,

260
00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:55,180
from 1879 to 1886.

261
00:20:55,840 --> 00:20:59,980
But after the age of 40, things began to go sour for the Wizard of Menlo Park.

262
00:21:00,780 --> 00:21:06,780
Thomas Edison comes to Menlo Park in 1876 at the age of about 29.

263
00:21:06,781 --> 00:21:10,780
At this point, he is really soaring as an inventor.

264
00:21:10,781 --> 00:21:14,780
Creative, innovative, everything he touches is incredible.

265
00:21:14,781 --> 00:21:18,780
You might say from about the age of 25 to about 40,

266
00:21:18,781 --> 00:21:21,780
he has this, what you might call, Midas touch.

267
00:21:21,781 --> 00:21:23,780
And it's not gold, it's platinum.

268
00:21:23,781 --> 00:21:26,780
And yet, there's a demarcation line.

269
00:21:26,781 --> 00:21:29,780
Right after that, all of a sudden, things that he touches

270
00:21:29,781 --> 00:21:32,780
aren't quite as successful, and it seems to get worse as time gets on.

271
00:21:32,781 --> 00:21:35,780
It kind of turns toward plate aluminum, you might say,

272
00:21:35,781 --> 00:21:37,680
you might say, in the later years.

273
00:21:38,580 --> 00:21:40,460
One of Edison's more bizarre failures

274
00:21:40,461 --> 00:21:42,760
involved manipulating electricity's powers

275
00:21:42,761 --> 00:21:46,000
to create a device for communicating with the dead.

276
00:21:49,100 --> 00:21:51,200
After the carnage of World War I,

277
00:21:51,400 --> 00:21:53,340
Edison felt families might be interested

278
00:21:53,341 --> 00:21:55,240
in contacting lost loved ones.

279
00:21:56,280 --> 00:21:57,740
Among his theories was the belief

280
00:21:57,741 --> 00:21:59,840
in an invisible source of electrical energy

281
00:21:59,841 --> 00:22:01,840
that could actually link survivors

282
00:22:01,841 --> 00:22:03,240
with people who had died.

283
00:22:04,240 --> 00:22:07,500
To date, no one has answered any of Edison's calls.

284
00:22:09,300 --> 00:22:12,180
Another Edison invention that failed was the talking doll,

285
00:22:12,660 --> 00:22:15,680
an idea that turned out to be way ahead of the day's technology.

286
00:22:17,280 --> 00:22:20,160
Edison invented the phonograph in 1877.

287
00:22:20,720 --> 00:22:23,420
In 1889, he decided to make a talking doll.

288
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:27,660
He imported the heads from Germany because they had the best bisque heads.

289
00:22:28,440 --> 00:22:31,980
On the dolls, he had a little phonograph motor, which was hand-driven.

290
00:22:31,981 --> 00:22:35,140
and a little wax record that would be placed around.

291
00:22:35,240 --> 00:22:38,040
There was a total of about seven or eight different songs that you could buy

292
00:22:38,041 --> 00:22:41,860
and would be placed inside the chest of this doll and be closed.

293
00:22:42,680 --> 00:22:44,520
And the children would crank the doll,

294
00:22:44,600 --> 00:22:47,340
but it was very difficult at the time to work this phonograph.

295
00:22:47,500 --> 00:22:49,260
First of all, you had to keep it at a constant speed,

296
00:22:49,440 --> 00:22:52,360
and you had to take the whole motor apart just to put a new record in.

297
00:22:57,580 --> 00:23:00,740
But the doll had two fatal flaws.

298
00:23:00,741 --> 00:23:03,880
The phonograph was too delicate for child's play.

299
00:23:03,881 --> 00:23:08,120
And the wax recordings wore out after only a few plays,

300
00:23:08,360 --> 00:23:11,580
leaving the metal stylus to grate against the steel cylinder.

301
00:23:12,300 --> 00:23:17,360
The result gave the doll a horrible screech that sent its tiny owners running.

302
00:23:18,440 --> 00:23:20,500
Edison folded the company in 1891.

303
00:23:22,860 --> 00:23:26,640
Another Edison idea that never got off, or in this case on the ground,

304
00:23:26,641 --> 00:23:30,920
was his design for a mass-producible house made entirely of concrete.

305
00:23:32,200 --> 00:23:37,000
The idea was not unrelated to the fact that Edison owned the Portland Cement Company,

306
00:23:37,460 --> 00:23:39,400
a major American supplier of concrete.

307
00:23:39,920 --> 00:23:42,440
Cement was very, very profitable to Thomas Edison.

308
00:23:42,900 --> 00:23:45,520
Yankee Stadium was made of Edison cement.

309
00:23:45,900 --> 00:23:48,520
The Panama Canal was made of Edison cement.

310
00:23:49,140 --> 00:23:53,640
So Edison was doing very well with cement, very profitable, a wonderful industry,

311
00:23:53,641 --> 00:23:57,220
and he started coming up with ideas what to do with cement.

312
00:23:58,160 --> 00:24:03,160
Edison stood to make a fortune if he could get Americans to fall in love with the idea of a concrete home.

313
00:24:03,680 --> 00:24:07,100
And to that end, he commissioned expensive and heavy cast iron molds

314
00:24:07,101 --> 00:24:10,380
of every part of a two-story, 1,500-square-foot house.

315
00:24:11,200 --> 00:24:13,940
All that would be needed to finish was plumbing and wiring.

316
00:24:14,720 --> 00:24:18,880
Edison even created an ill-fated line of concrete furniture for his homes,

317
00:24:19,260 --> 00:24:20,740
including couches and chairs.

318
00:24:21,520 --> 00:24:23,300
As soon as the press heard about it,

319
00:24:23,640 --> 00:24:28,280
they had cartoons with a two-horse team connected to your couch every time you wanted the dust.

320
00:24:29,260 --> 00:24:32,020
And Edison actually liked the cartoon. He hung it up in his office.

321
00:24:32,820 --> 00:24:36,120
In a day when the average house sold for a few thousand dollars,

322
00:24:36,121 --> 00:24:40,700
the concrete house carried a heavy price tag of over $100,000.

323
00:24:41,660 --> 00:24:44,500
Mass production would be the only way to keep the homes affordable.

324
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:50,560
Edison's cement works produced only 11 homes before going bankrupt.

325
00:24:50,740 --> 00:24:58,500
Edison wasn't the only well-known inventor to come up with an original and ill-fated idea for mass housing.

326
00:24:59,320 --> 00:25:02,880
Buckminster Fuller was a highly regarded engineer and veteran futurist.

327
00:25:03,960 --> 00:25:09,300
Michigan's Henry Ford Museum contains the only existing Fuller-designed Dymaxion house.

328
00:25:10,900 --> 00:25:16,100
Fuller's failed dream of lighter, cheaper housing is being reconstructed for public display.

329
00:25:16,101 --> 00:25:22,560
After World War II, there was everybody and his brother trying to get in and make a buck off the housing shortage.

330
00:25:22,700 --> 00:25:26,880
But Fuller had a totally different idea to revolutionize the housing industry.

331
00:25:27,060 --> 00:25:35,340
Both factory production and a completely new engineering concept, the Damaxian house, which stood for dynamic maximum tension.

332
00:25:36,320 --> 00:25:42,920
Unlike most homes, which sit on a foundation, Fuller felt that certain materials performed better in tension.

333
00:25:43,880 --> 00:25:49,080
The entire house is suspended by wires hung from a central mast, much like an umbrella.

334
00:25:50,120 --> 00:25:51,380
Nothing rests on the ground.

335
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:58,060
Fuller turned to an aircraft manufacturing firm to produce a lightweight and easy-to-ship aluminum exterior.

336
00:25:59,080 --> 00:26:01,680
The entire home could be delivered in a single tube.

337
00:26:02,340 --> 00:26:06,700
The cost of a new Dymaxion house was $6,500.

338
00:26:07,600 --> 00:26:12,400
The two bedrooms each had their own tiny prefab bathroom cubes.

339
00:26:12,920 --> 00:26:15,160
It's not what we think of as a bathroom today.

340
00:26:15,960 --> 00:26:17,640
It's a little bit close in there, folks.

341
00:26:18,320 --> 00:26:23,300
As well as imaginative revolving shelving units to maximize the restricted space.

342
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:30,440
Like Fuller's later geodesic dome home, the Dymaxion failed in part because it was so different.

343
00:26:31,180 --> 00:26:35,480
Its one-size-fits-all floor plan made add-ons impossible for growing families.

344
00:26:36,420 --> 00:26:39,580
The house's round shape also gave homeowners headaches,

345
00:26:40,120 --> 00:26:44,940
trying to make square bookcases and couches fit into their new, round world.

346
00:26:46,160 --> 00:26:51,580
Like many others, Fuller's Dymaxion house may have also failed because he was just a bit ahead of his time.

347
00:26:53,540 --> 00:26:59,500
While some failed inventions tried making life easier, others came close to destroying it.

348
00:27:00,040 --> 00:27:02,860
Up next, a jaw-dropping glass of water.

349
00:27:02,861 --> 00:27:04,890
In the year 2000, the U.S.

350
00:27:04,891 --> 00:27:10,120
Patents Office received more than 175,000 applications for patents.

351
00:27:10,820 --> 00:27:14,740
43% of the applications filed failed to obtain approval.

352
00:27:15,780 --> 00:27:18,300
Failed Inventions will return on Modern Marvels.

353
00:27:19,860 --> 00:27:23,780
The field of medicine has produced the lion's share of failed inventions,

354
00:27:24,340 --> 00:27:29,640
and Bob McCoy has amassed a collection of more than 250,000 bizarre gadgets

355
00:27:29,641 --> 00:27:34,100
at his Minneapolis-based Museum of Questionable Medical Devices.

356
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:38,540
The discovery in 1898 of the radioactive element of radium

357
00:27:38,541 --> 00:27:41,320
spawned dozens of lethal medical inventions.

358
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:45,380
People wonder why there are so many radium inventions.

359
00:27:46,300 --> 00:27:50,440
I think it's because it was a popular thing in the beginning of the 20th century.

360
00:27:50,900 --> 00:27:53,100
It had a certain sense of vitality and energy.

361
00:27:53,840 --> 00:27:57,700
Not only did they sell radium products thinking they're going to make you healthy,

362
00:27:57,701 --> 00:28:00,300
They also tied it in with sexuality.

363
00:28:00,800 --> 00:28:04,320
They were making radioactive athletic supporters for weak, sagging men.

364
00:28:04,760 --> 00:28:10,360
They were drying out boars, testicles, and making them radioactive to improve your romantic talent.

365
00:28:10,980 --> 00:28:15,300
They were making radium suppositories for men or women.

366
00:28:15,960 --> 00:28:18,600
A whole line of goofy stuff like this were being sold.

367
00:28:19,300 --> 00:28:23,400
Fast talkers attributed all manner of benefits to this hot fad.

368
00:28:24,060 --> 00:28:29,760
This was called the Cosmos Radioactive Pad, marketed to people who had arthritis,

369
00:28:29,960 --> 00:28:33,420
with a promise that it relieved the pain of arthritis.

370
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:38,540
Oddly enough, in the back it says, warning, do not open this and eat the contents of this bag.

371
00:28:39,060 --> 00:28:40,520
Who would want to eat a bag of radium?

372
00:28:41,380 --> 00:28:44,680
In the beginning of the 20th century, radium was big business.

373
00:28:45,260 --> 00:28:47,500
It was imported, for the most part, from Europe.

374
00:28:48,200 --> 00:28:51,140
Here is a grocery store selling radioactive water,

375
00:28:51,300 --> 00:28:54,880
claiming you have the invisible hand of Mother Nature in your body to make you healthy.

376
00:28:55,440 --> 00:28:59,960
All of this was going fine until a fellow in Pittsburgh began marketing

377
00:28:59,961 --> 00:29:04,740
these little bottles of Radithor to improve your romantic talent.

378
00:29:04,741 --> 00:29:08,000
A man in Pittsburgh drank 1,400 bottles of this.

379
00:29:08,420 --> 00:29:09,920
His upper and lower jaw fell off.

380
00:29:10,480 --> 00:29:16,280
That's when they realized this radium cannot be sold willy-nilly, that it was dangerous.

381
00:29:17,060 --> 00:29:22,400
Like radium, the invention of the X-ray also spawned a host of potentially lethal creations.

382
00:29:23,020 --> 00:29:29,880
The X-ray is discovered in 1896 and put into use almost immediately, and it seems miraculous to people.

383
00:29:30,300 --> 00:29:34,360
But then, as often happens with these technologies, they get a little warped,

384
00:29:34,520 --> 00:29:38,040
and people begin loading on more expectations on top of them.

385
00:29:38,480 --> 00:29:45,200
Things snowball and go skidding off the road and become ludicrous at best and sometimes lethal at worst.

386
00:29:46,280 --> 00:29:51,965
Case in point, in the 1940s and 50s, shoe stores all over the country and the U.S.

387
00:29:51,966 --> 00:29:52,666
military

388
00:29:52,667 --> 00:29:57,620
were using the Adrian X-Ray shoe-fitting machines to achieve exact sizings.

389
00:29:58,220 --> 00:30:04,720
The appeal was to see your own toes wiggling in your shoes and seeing, oh, those are my toes,

390
00:30:05,140 --> 00:30:10,020
and to see if your shoes were too narrow or too short or too wide or too long.

391
00:30:10,021 --> 00:30:14,920
The company in Milwaukee produced about 10,000 of these things.

392
00:30:15,860 --> 00:30:18,100
And even though they were lead-lined,

393
00:30:18,780 --> 00:30:23,460
there was no lead shielding around either the ports into which you put your feet

394
00:30:23,461 --> 00:30:25,840
or the various viewing ports.

395
00:30:26,220 --> 00:30:29,980
It had three of them, one for the customer, one for the shoe salesman,

396
00:30:30,380 --> 00:30:33,800
and one for parents in case they were buying shoes for their child.

397
00:30:34,500 --> 00:30:37,620
The amount of X-ray leakage is rather alarming.

398
00:30:37,621 --> 00:30:49,941
As a comic book enthusiast, I'm rather perplexed why we didn't have super-powered shoe salesmen as a result of all the rampant radiation bombarding these fellows that

399
00:30:49,942 --> 00:30:51,500
worked in the stores.

400
00:30:52,320 --> 00:30:58,060
Once radiation leakage was confirmed in 1950, the government ordered the machines destroyed.

401
00:30:58,061 --> 00:31:04,240
the arrival of electricity in homes generated a slew of new medical appliances

402
00:31:04,241 --> 00:31:12,180
none of them with more dubious value than the prostate gland warmer it was patented in 1918

403
00:31:12,181 --> 00:31:18,600
it's supposed to excite a man's abdominal brain did you know men had abdominal brains the u.s

404
00:31:18,601 --> 00:31:24,760
patent office says we do now the wattage of this bulb controls how warm this will be this is a 25

405
00:31:24,761 --> 00:31:27,600
and this doesn't get too hot.

406
00:31:28,140 --> 00:31:29,980
Actually, this didn't do anything.

407
00:31:31,960 --> 00:31:35,440
Electronic medical gadgets weren't the only inventions that failed

408
00:31:35,441 --> 00:31:37,440
because they didn't live up to expectations.

409
00:31:38,340 --> 00:31:42,300
Nearly 50 years later, inventors had shifted their focus from medicine

410
00:31:42,301 --> 00:31:45,320
to gadgets we find every day around our houses.

411
00:31:46,340 --> 00:31:49,260
At a 1977 Polaroid stockholders meeting,

412
00:31:49,720 --> 00:31:53,080
President Edwin Land, the inventor of the Polaroid Instant Camera,

413
00:31:53,081 --> 00:31:56,820
proudly unveiled his latest creation, Polavision.

414
00:31:58,320 --> 00:31:59,720
Dubbed a movie in a minute,

415
00:32:00,260 --> 00:32:04,200
Land said Polavision's home movies would become a part of your diary.

416
00:32:05,720 --> 00:32:08,100
Utilizing a Polaroid-like developing system,

417
00:32:08,520 --> 00:32:11,260
Polavision used short two-minute recording cartridges.

418
00:32:12,640 --> 00:32:14,560
Each cartridge cost $10

419
00:32:14,561 --> 00:32:19,040
and needed a special TV set to develop the Polaroid movie film.

420
00:32:20,520 --> 00:32:22,520
Polavision did not record sound.

421
00:32:23,080 --> 00:32:31,600
As Land addressed his stockholders, he wasn't banking on the success of the new Sony Betamax and JVC VHS video formats.

422
00:32:35,880 --> 00:32:42,060
Consumers opted for the longer recording times, sound, and instantaneous playback of video.

423
00:32:42,540 --> 00:32:46,920
Polaroid lost over $65 million in research and marketing costs.

424
00:32:46,921 --> 00:32:56,920
While video systems rendered Polavision useless, the Society of Unuseless Inventions welcomes the near misses.

425
00:32:56,921 --> 00:33:02,920
An unuseless invention is something that is useless in the sense that it doesn't work.

426
00:33:02,921 --> 00:33:05,920
It's not the sort of invention that will make your life necessarily easier.

427
00:33:05,921 --> 00:33:07,939
But it's not entirely useless.

428
00:33:07,940 --> 00:33:12,920
It's almost useful or it's unuseless because it gives amusement to people.

429
00:33:12,921 --> 00:33:20,920
This is the honest husband hat and the idea is that a lady can give this to her husband or her boyfriend

430
00:33:20,921 --> 00:33:23,920
and assure that when he walks through the park he's not going to look at all the pretty girls.

431
00:33:23,921 --> 00:33:30,920
He's got a picture of the wife and kids right here to remind him of his romantic obligations.

432
00:33:30,921 --> 00:33:40,920
This is the AC free battery charger and the notion is that you can recharge one of your old batteries simply by using 12 new batteries.

433
00:33:40,921 --> 00:33:44,660
It does really work, but you'll also shorten the lives of these 12 new batteries,

434
00:33:44,980 --> 00:33:48,460
so what you need is another 144 batteries in order to pump these back up.

435
00:33:49,540 --> 00:33:51,500
This is the back scratcher's T-shirt.

436
00:33:51,780 --> 00:33:56,320
If you've ever had that problem of having to tell your partner higher, lower, to the left or the right,

437
00:33:56,480 --> 00:34:00,140
you know that it would be so much more convenient if you could just say,

438
00:34:00,240 --> 00:34:01,340
please scratch F3.

439
00:34:03,120 --> 00:34:06,340
Oh, oh yeah, oh, good, good.

440
00:34:06,341 --> 00:34:17,220
The unuseless inventions are a way of celebrating the fact that failure is just as important as success to the development of future technology.

441
00:34:17,640 --> 00:34:25,400
And today, with all the technology we have in the 21st century, we're standing as much on the back of past failure as we are past success.

442
00:34:26,200 --> 00:34:32,040
Plenty of failed inventions have affected the useful and necessary items that touch our everyday lives.

443
00:34:32,880 --> 00:34:36,360
Next, failed visions of lifestyles of the future.

444
00:34:37,200 --> 00:34:42,460
In 2000, the FDA halted production of the stimulator, a supposed cure-all for muscular ailments.

445
00:34:42,820 --> 00:34:46,320
Over 800,000 stimulators were sold at a cost of $65 million.

446
00:34:47,280 --> 00:34:49,980
Failed Inventions will return on Modern Marvelous.

447
00:34:52,520 --> 00:34:56,440
Most failed inventions have to do with the everyday things in our lives.

448
00:34:56,441 --> 00:35:01,420
The food that we eat, the clothes that we wear, the gadgets we have lying around the house.

449
00:35:02,040 --> 00:35:06,980
and even as Buckminster Fuller discovered, are homes themselves.

450
00:35:06,981 --> 00:35:10,280
Ann Arbor, Michigan's new ProductWorks Research Center

451
00:35:10,281 --> 00:35:14,180
certainly looks like a supermarket, but it isn't.

452
00:35:14,181 --> 00:35:18,180
In fact, this is a place where failed products come to die.

453
00:35:18,181 --> 00:35:23,500
To date, more than 80,000 of them have come here as their final resting place.

454
00:35:23,501 --> 00:35:27,420
Every year, more than 30,000 new products hit the shelves,

455
00:35:27,421 --> 00:35:31,980
and every year, more than 90% of them fail.

456
00:35:31,981 --> 00:35:38,520
An expensive proposition considering supermarket products like these can cost up to $20 to $50 million to launch.

457
00:35:40,420 --> 00:35:46,220
Robert McMath created this space in 1981 to help manufacturers launch new products.

458
00:35:46,860 --> 00:35:52,700
One of the things the collection represents is learning lessons from the past,

459
00:35:53,260 --> 00:35:58,760
and there are very positive lessons to learn from mistakes as well as the successes.

460
00:35:59,320 --> 00:36:01,680
We like to say we offer success insurance.

461
00:36:01,980 --> 00:36:12,420
Bad packaging, bad names, and just plain bad ideas can all add up to be concept killers.

462
00:36:12,421 --> 00:36:13,700
This is an interesting product.

463
00:36:13,701 --> 00:36:15,200
This is Barbecue Buddies.

464
00:36:15,201 --> 00:36:20,100
This was a nice barbecue sauce you sprayed on and always thought this was a great idea.

465
00:36:20,101 --> 00:36:24,780
The unfortunate problem is the package is so tall on its own before you even put the sprayer

466
00:36:24,781 --> 00:36:27,220
on that it doesn't fit on the average shelf.

467
00:36:27,221 --> 00:36:28,780
You can't put it on a supermarket shelf.

468
00:36:28,781 --> 00:36:30,260
You have to put it up at the top.

469
00:36:30,261 --> 00:36:32,820
It also doesn't fit in the refrigerator.

470
00:36:32,821 --> 00:36:38,540
Avert is a great concept, but unfortunately, they refer to it as a virucidal tissue, and

471
00:36:38,541 --> 00:36:40,660
that kind of got people scared.

472
00:36:40,661 --> 00:36:44,120
They think of homicidal and whatever else, and they weren't quite sure what a virucidal

473
00:36:44,121 --> 00:36:47,280
tissue was.

474
00:36:47,281 --> 00:36:48,680
This was an interesting product.

475
00:36:48,681 --> 00:36:54,120
It is actually a scrambled egg type product on a stick, and you can push it up.

476
00:36:54,121 --> 00:36:58,600
So it's a very convenient product for taking with you, perhaps in the car.

477
00:36:58,601 --> 00:37:01,740
The unfortunate problem is that it fell apart if you pushed it up wrong,

478
00:37:01,840 --> 00:37:05,060
and people were complaining in the cars that the eggs were falling in their lap.

479
00:37:05,840 --> 00:37:12,440
In the 1980s, a nationwide health craze spawned a number of clean products that hit the supermarket aisles.

480
00:37:12,980 --> 00:37:17,180
It also saw one of the biggest supermarket product failures of all time.

481
00:37:17,780 --> 00:37:23,420
The notion was a smokeless cigarette wouldn't involve burning tobacco,

482
00:37:23,421 --> 00:37:30,720
but rather an element that would produce the flavor of tobacco in your mouth through a plastic tube.

483
00:37:31,400 --> 00:37:34,440
The device was extremely difficult to light.

484
00:37:35,180 --> 00:37:40,160
Then sucking on this thing, they said, was enough to promote a hernia.

485
00:37:40,500 --> 00:37:43,820
The taste of it was compared to burning lettuce.

486
00:37:44,200 --> 00:37:50,140
Even the president of R.J. Reynolds admitted that the premier tasted like crap.

487
00:37:50,960 --> 00:37:56,740
For many outsiders, the arrival of the Premier seemed like a kind of admission from Reynolds' executives.

488
00:37:57,840 --> 00:38:02,460
The cigarette companies did not want to acknowledge that there were any health dangers of cigarettes themselves.

489
00:38:03,060 --> 00:38:07,180
What they hit upon was to describe it as a clean smoking experience.

490
00:38:07,181 --> 00:38:13,320
But at the same time, they didn't want to acknowledge that their other brands were dirty or unclean.

491
00:38:13,321 --> 00:38:20,620
With no smoke to blow or ashes to flick, the premier cigarette robbed smokers of much of the smoking ritual.

492
00:38:21,560 --> 00:38:29,340
Consumer disinterest quickly extinguished the idea, costing Reynolds more than $300 million in research and marketing costs.

493
00:38:30,660 --> 00:38:33,100
Failed inventions haven't just affected the food we eat.

494
00:38:33,480 --> 00:38:38,100
They've touched every part of our daily lives, right down to the clothes on our backs.

495
00:38:38,900 --> 00:38:40,400
They're being clothes made from lava.

496
00:38:40,401 --> 00:38:45,640
volcanic eruptions may one day become the source of a whole line of earthy fashions.

497
00:38:47,060 --> 00:38:50,380
And even a slew of fresh styles straight from the farm.

498
00:38:51,420 --> 00:38:56,900
Would you believe this transparent raincoat made in 1955 came from surplus fat from pork chops?

499
00:38:57,520 --> 00:39:00,200
But the paper dress ripped the competition.

500
00:39:01,080 --> 00:39:06,100
The paper dress is one of those almost uniquely 60s phenomena.

501
00:39:06,101 --> 00:39:11,640
speaks a lot about the kind of carefree go-go days.

502
00:39:12,780 --> 00:39:15,380
The Scott Paper Company manufactured the first dresses

503
00:39:15,381 --> 00:39:17,600
to promote a new line of napkins and towels.

504
00:39:18,900 --> 00:39:21,980
The stains could simply be erased or trimmed away.

505
00:39:23,260 --> 00:39:25,400
Ultimately, the paper dress lost favor

506
00:39:25,401 --> 00:39:28,180
because its one-size-fits-all soured women

507
00:39:28,181 --> 00:39:31,080
who didn't appreciate its billowy, muumuu-style cut.

508
00:39:31,920 --> 00:39:34,420
But where to put all those paper dresses?

509
00:39:34,421 --> 00:39:36,260
and grocery store flops.

510
00:39:37,020 --> 00:39:40,080
Creating a vision of tomorrow and the homes we'll live in

511
00:39:40,081 --> 00:39:44,460
has proved to be an uphill battle that most inventors never quite get right.

512
00:39:44,540 --> 00:39:47,780
Towering buildings and bright lights of the city of tomorrow,

513
00:39:48,360 --> 00:39:51,580
where plazas of urban living rise over freeways

514
00:39:51,581 --> 00:39:53,680
and vehicles electronically paced,

515
00:39:54,220 --> 00:39:57,240
travel routes safely, swiftly, efficiently.

516
00:39:58,180 --> 00:40:02,840
Any kind of projection of the future is a statement about present realities.

517
00:40:02,841 --> 00:40:06,620
the city of tomorrow will be clean and spacious.

518
00:40:06,940 --> 00:40:10,240
Well, that's because the city of today, in other words, they're saying,

519
00:40:10,440 --> 00:40:14,720
is crowded and awful and depressing.

520
00:40:16,260 --> 00:40:20,480
Inventors have long claimed the house of the future will come with a robot or two

521
00:40:20,481 --> 00:40:22,780
that frees man from the workaday grind.

522
00:40:24,060 --> 00:40:26,380
The future will be full of free time.

523
